ason":"For radius r, the inscribed square has diagonal 2r and area 2r², so the circle-minus-square area is (π-2)r². Equal shading gives π-2=(π-2)R²/4. Hence R²=4 and, since the radius is positive, R=2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["math:similar-figure-area-scaling"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"2025_AMC_8_Q18","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p079","cnm_p188","cnm_075"],"id":"solution:area-scaling:a34777b8a716","label":"Compare corresponding areas","methodDirectNodeIds":{"area-scaling":["cnm_p150","math:similar-figure-area-scaling"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_p150","math:similar-figure-area-scaling","cnm_p079","cnm_p188","cnm_075"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"alignmentIds":["alignment:28cfdf77a35aeadb383ef95d"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A (4.5); the maximizing values are c=±2√5.","reason":"Write the roots as u±iv, where u=c/2 and u²+v²=10. Their reciprocals are (u∓iv)/10, so the trapezoid area is 99|u|v/100. This is maximal when u²=v²=5, giving c=±2√5. Both maximizing values are closest to 4.5 among the listed choices.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["math:similar-figure-area-scaling"],"coverage":"partial","flags":["No sign restriction on c is stated, so the maximizing parameter is not unique. Both maximizing values nevertheless lead to choice A."],"questionId":"2022_AMC_12A_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"similar-triangles-area","label":"Maximize a larger triangle","methodDirectNodeIds":{"similar-triangles-area":["cnm_h098","math:conjugate-polynomial-roots","cnm_226","math:similar-figure-area-scaling","cnm_p148","cnm_174"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h098","math:conjugate-polynomial-roots","cnm_226","math:similar-figure-area-scaling","cnm_p148","cnm_174"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"alignmentIds":["alignment:28cfdf77a35aeadb383ef95d"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E. 4 + 2√2","reason":"